Document is in good shape — the density stratification closing the loop on Aim 3 is exactly what was needed, and Section 7 is unusually honest for a working document. Three things.

1. Your positive control is underpowered relative to your null, and that's attackable.

ChromHMM_bivalent n=46, Roadmap_silenced n=41 — the two sets carrying the whole positive-control argument are the smallest in the table. dbSUPER is n=158. So you're claiming "we detect an effect at n=41 and find nothing at n=158," which reads backwards to a reviewer even though it's fine statistically.

Two cheap fixes, either works:

Report the minimum detectable effect at n=158 for the SE test. If you could have detected d=0.25 at that n with 80% power, the null is informative and you can say so in one sentence.
Or subsample SE to n=45 across many draws and show the positive controls still exceed it.

The first is one line and it's what I'd do. Without it, "we found nothing for SE" is vulnerable to "you weren't looking hard enough" — even though you were looking harder there than anywhere.

2. Lambert_TF is your best-powered real effect and it's being underused.

n=156, d=+0.46, p=0.0005, survives magnitude correction. That's larger n than either positive control, comparable effect size, and — critically — TFs are a mechanism-defined set (DNA-binding domain), not an expression list.

That makes it a better positive control than silenced or bivalent: well-powered, mechanism-based, and it separates. It also partly answers the Eisenberg problem in advance, since it shows mechanism-defined sets can separate where expression-defined ones don't. Worth foregrounding in Section 4 rather than leaving mid-table.

3. LOEUF deserves more prominence in Section 2.

4.08 SD is the standout margin by some distance, and it's the one target where shape does nearly all the work (0.210 vs 0.191 amount-corrected). "Contact architecture beyond magnitude predicts evolutionary constraint" is a substantive, quotable claim and it's currently buried in a table. The 6-of-9-inside-1-SD honesty is right, but don't let it flatten the one result that's genuinely strong.

Everything else reads well. The retraction table in Section 7 is the kind of thing that makes examiners trust a document rather than doubt it.